      <subsection name="Description" id="ExceptionWrappingShouldUseContext_Description">
        <div>
          Checks that exception wrapping includes context variables.
        </div>

        <p>
          Rationale: When catching an exception and re-throwing a new exception that wraps
          the caught exception, it is recommended to include contextual information in the
          new exception constructor (such as method parameters, local variables, or fields).
          Failing to include context makes troubleshooting and debugging production issues
          significantly harder.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <subsection name="Examples" id="ExceptionWrappingShouldUseContext_Examples">
        <p id="Example1-config">
          To configure the check:
        </p>
        <div class="wrapper"><pre class="prettyprint"><code class="language-xml">
&lt;module name="Checker"&gt;
  &lt;module name="TreeWalker"&gt;
    &lt;module name="ExceptionWrappingShouldUseContext"/&gt;
  &lt;/module&gt;
&lt;/module&gt;
</code></pre></div>
        <p id="Example1-code">
          Example of violations
        </p>
        <div class="wrapper"><pre class="prettyprint"><code class="language-java">
class Example1 {
  void method1(String someParameter) {
    try {
      riskyOperation();
    }
    catch (IOException ex) {
      // violation below 'Exception wrapping should always use context variables.'
      throw new RuntimeException("unable to process ", ex);
    }
  }

  void method2(String someParameter) {
    try {
      riskyOperation();
    }
    catch (IOException ex) {
      // ok, uses 'someParameter' in the exception constructor
      throw new RuntimeException("unable to process " + someParameter, ex);
    }
  }

  private void riskyOperation() throws IOException {
  }
}
</code></pre></div>
      </subsection>

